From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 14:06:33 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 120/160] fix: the camera drove render state while the level
 was still being built

CRASH

Opening a level whose Cinematic Camera has Scene Referred saved ON froze the
editor (unrelated: 25 redeployed shaders had invalidated the whole shader cache,
so every shader recompiled) and then killed the RenderThread with
EXCEPTION_ACCESS_VIOLATION reading 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF, twice in a row, one second
after the last level-load line.

WHAT THE LOG ACTUALLY SAYS - and the log line that lied

  <13:53:09> r_SceneReferred=0 [  ]
  <13:53:12> general.open
  <13:53:17> Precaching textures done in 0.16 seconds
  <13:53:17> [SceneReferred] request flipped -> off, pipeline 'Standard_0' ...
  <13:53:18> [crash]

That "request flipped -> off" was read as "the request was ON during the load and
the engine reset it at load end". It was not. m_nSceneReferredLogged starts at -1
and a level load builds a NEW CGraphicsPipelineResources, so the first Update() of
the level always printed a flip that never happened - here with r_SceneReferred
already 0, i.e. with the request never having been on at all. The seeding pass is
now silent when the answer is "off" and says "pipeline '...' starts scene-referred"
when it is not, so the next crash log means what it says.

ROOT CAUSE

The Cinematic Camera drives shared render state from ENTITY_EVENT_UPDATE and
ENTITY_EVENT_COMPONENT_PROPERTY_CHANGED, and the only gate on it was ownership
(CanDriveRenderState: am I the active camera, am I not a preview). Neither event
knows whether a level is being torn down or built, and every Apply* behind that
gate reaches into something the load owns:

  * the post-effect bus - which CRenderer::EF_ResetPostEffects clears on the
    render thread at the end of the load, so anything published during the load is
    thrown away and the switch is flipped twice before the first frame is drawn;
  * the renderer's texture set - ApplyDisplayLuts uploads two 3D LUT textures
    whose ids the tone map then holds, while the level load is freeing and
    re-creating render resources ("*** Start clearing render resources ***"
    precedes "Ending game context" in the same load);
  * the time of day - PublishSceneReferredConvention forces
    ITimeOfDay::Update(true, true), which recomputes the whole Nishita sky dome.

That is the class of bug: our work runs on a pipeline whose resources are still
being created, and against a texture set that is being replaced underneath it.

THE FIX - the trodden path

CanDriveRenderState() now also requires ESYSTEM_GLOBAL_STATE == RUNNING, which is
the engine's own "the level is up" answer: CCryEditDoc::LoadLevel() sets it right
after firing ESYSTEM_EVENT_LEVEL_LOAD_END, CCryAction after
LEVEL_GAMEPLAY_START. A poll, not a listener, so there is no bookkeeping to get
out of step and a host that fires a begin without an end cannot leave the camera
stuck. The plugin's UpdateBeforeFinalizeCamera() takes the same gate one level up,
so the resolver does not hand the render state around mid-load either. Nothing is
applied during a load; the first frame after it applies everything, so the switch
comes on by itself one frame late with the level fully built.

The plugin's existing system-event listener brackets that:

  LEVEL_UNLOAD_START / LEVEL_UNLOAD / LEVEL_POST_UNLOAD / LEVEL_LOAD_PREPARE /
  LEVEL_LOAD_START -> OnLevelTeardown()
      takes the request and the four LUT params off the bus, writes
      E3DPARAM_SCENE_REFERRED to 0 as a PLAIN store (no forced TOD update - the
      level is going away and reloads its own time of day), releases both LUT
      slots while their texture ids are still ours, and drops every captured
      baseline, which was taken from a bus that is about to be reset.

  LEVEL_LOAD_END / LEVEL_GAMEPLAY_START -> OnLevelLoaded()
      forgets the LUT path cache and the baselines, so the new level re-applies
      from scratch instead of from a cache that predates the load.

TWO REAL HAZARDS FOUND IN THE AUDIT, BOTH FIXED

ClearLutSlot released a bare texture id. IRenderer::RemoveTexture(id) is
GetByID(id) followed by Release() - it trusts the id - and a level change frees
render resources BEFORE the entities are destroyed, so by the time OnShutDown gets
there the id can already belong to somebody else's texture; releasing it drops a
stranger's reference and leaves a dangling CTexture for the render thread. The
slot now records the cache name it uploaded under ($ccamODT / $ccamLMT) and only
releases an id whose texture still carries it. The in-place resize path went
through the same unguarded RemoveTexture and now goes through ClearLutSlot.

CAutoExposureStage cached m_meterHistogramUAV across the life of the CTexture, and
EnsureMeterHistogramTexture() returned early - without re-deriving it - whenever
the texture object existed. A ResourceViewHandle is an INDEX into the owning
CDeviceTexture's view container, not a pointer, and the CTexture object outlives
its device texture (a level change frees render resources and the pool re-creates
them under the same object). Used against the new device texture the handle
indexes past the end of a container holding only the predefined views, and
CComposedDeviceObjectStorage::Lookup()'s bounds assert is compiled out in Profile,
so ClearUAV was handed whatever lay past the vector. The handle is now re-derived
whenever the device texture underneath moves (one pointer compare on the common
path) and a null view skips the clear instead of being dereferenced.

ALSO

A flip of the switch to off now stops any running EXR capture, so the S6 ring
cannot be left with a download in flight against a path that has been turned off.

Byte identity off the switch is unchanged: every line above is either inside
scene-referred-only code, inside the ccam plugin, or a log string.

CryRenderD3D11 and CinematicCamera build 0 errors / 0 warnings outside Code/SDKs.
No shader changed.

